# CONTROL for print-as-statement.py. The same shape at the same position with
# a different name, and it is right.
#
#   call [0, 6)
#     function: identifier  "log"
#     arguments: argument_list [3, 6)
#
# Both trees, identical. A call in statement position is not the problem; the
# problem is one identifier for which python.json carries a second, competing
# statement rule. Without this control the guilty witness would be consistent
# with joints misreading every bare call in a module, and it does not.
roots 1
mends 0
holds call
holds argument_list
lacks print_statement
spans call 0 6
